 president  unlike any other and that's true  but trump is not an aberration of  history rather he is a product of  american history  and of a democracy that has become more  partisan and more polarized with each  passing year  republicans and democrats have always  had different views but the gap between  them  has never been as wide as it is now this  is the memorial to republican president  dwight d eisenhower in the 50s he had an  88 approval rating among party faithful  remarkably nearly half of all democrats  approved of him as well  now you could chalk that up to the fact  that the economy was booming in the  post-war era  but then take a look at this in 1956  eisenhower got almost 40 percent of the  african-american vote  but by the 1960s following the passage  of the civil rights act by democrat  lyndon johnson  that supported plummeted to just 6  percent  under nixon republicans used the  so-called southern strategy  veiled racist messaging to attract  disgruntled white voters in the south  the seeds of trumpism were planted  decades ago  and it's over race where the divisions  are the most clear  before 1964 african-american voters were  distributed among the two parties  but over time a gap developed as  non-white voters steadily and  dramatically  shifted to the democratic party today  those divisions are most striking in the  rust belt areas to the north where  white rural voters some of them lifelong  democrats  came out in droves in 2016 and gave  president  trump the white house and that's why  wisconsin  michigan and pennsylvania will be key  battlegrounds this election  foreign asser global news washington  up next from 2016 to 2020 how the last  four years  influenced how these americans will vote  we're back from washington after a break  welcome back to washington our reporter  mike armstrong has been on the road in  the u.s for a couple of weeks now  observing all the public health  restrictions of course  and he's talked to all kinds of peopl